uh to tell you the truth um tell me  though how  how much influence do you really have as  an advisor how much of  all of that information that you get  gets through to the politician  well that's a a really good question  because  we science advisors have to learn that  we have just to present  the data to repres to present evidence  it and to  uh suggest things but at the end we are  not the ones taking the decisions  we're just there trying to the  politician to make  a better or the best decision that he or  she can make  so it's kind of a difficult thing for  example for me it has been really  um i have a lot of of influence for  example in diagnostics or  or how we handle the disease or how we  understand the virus or what's happening  with all these new variants as you said  before  but um the the actual measures like for  example lockdown or economical  consequence having economical  consequences  it's part of what the politician should  do so i'm not  that powerful but um i think  that for at least here in costa rica the  government has been really good  opening for us the scientists this  slot so that we can go through and  suggest  what about the message of masks then  some scientists reportedly  played that down to prevent a mass  public rush on masks and ensure supplies  to medical staff what went wrong there  well i think we were naive actually all  of us that  used to study coronaviruses before we  were  um we knew about coronavirus sars  one and we knew that we it we were able  to control it so when  all the cases were starting to come up  in china  we thought that maybe well this is this  looks like stars  maybe it's something similar maybe we  can control it  in the same measures that we used  in 2002 and 2003 and i think we were  kind of hoping and naive that  thinking that it was going to be like  tsar's one but we were surprised  actually  we we we knew  at that moment after wuhan went into  lockdown and after china went into  lockdown  that it 